{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>I am El Shaddai.</b> Shaddai — sufficient in His divinity for every creature; this is the language of Rashi, of blessed memory." "<b>And Rabbenu Hananel, of blessed memory, wrote</b> that the commandment of circumcision was given in this name because a person's strength fails through circumcision, as the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded on (Proverbs 2:7): ״יצפון לישרים תושיה״ — 'He stores up soundness for the upright' — this is circumcision, which the Holy One, blessed be He, stored away for twenty generations; for from Adam to Abraham was twenty generations. And this name is mentioned in connection with might, as it is written (Ezekiel 1:24): ״כקול מים רבים כקול שדי״ — 'like the voice of many waters, like the voice of Shaddai.' And lest you say: since he cuts his flesh, his strength has failed and his enemies will rise against him, therefore He said to him, ״אני אל שדי״ — 'I am El Shaddai': I supply you with enough strength and might that you may prevail over your enemies, you and all your circumcised seed." "<b>And the Rambam, of blessed memory, explained</b> that He has no need, in His existence, for that which exists, nor in the continuance of the existence of anything besides Himself; rather, His existence suffices in and of itself." "<b>And the correct interpretation</b> of this name is what was explained in the name of the Nagid, of blessed memory: that it derives from the root שודד, meaning 'one who despoils and vanquishes the arrays of the stars.' And it appears to me that because of this, Scripture attributed despoiling (שוד) to this name, as Isaiah mentioned (Isaiah 13:6): ״כשוד משדי יבא״ — 'as destruction from Shaddai shall it come.'" "<b>And the Gaon Rabbenu Saadya</b> explained that its meaning is from the expression די מחסורו — 'sufficient for his need' — meaning that He supplies the lack of all existing beings, all of them. And they further interpreted it as being from the language of (Isaiah 66:23) ״מדי חדש בחדשו״, (I Samuel 1:7) ״מדי עלותו בבית ה׳״ — which is language applied to a thing when it is recurring; so its meaning regarding the Holy One, blessed be He, would be everlastingness, like the Name of Being (הויה), which is language of everlastingness." "<b>And the Ramban</b> explained this: that it is the attribute of Gevurah (Might) that governs the world, which the Sages call 'the attribute of judgment below.' And the reason He mentioned this name in connection with circumcision is that He made known to him that He is El Shaddai — that is, mighty (תקיף), who constrains (משדד) and overpowers his star, so that he would beget children. For it is known that by this name the hidden miracles are performed. And Abraham, whose nature and star decreed that he would not beget children — this name nullified the power of the star so that he would beget. This is a hidden miracle, not publicized before onlookers, for they would say: it is the nature of human beings to beget children, and there are many who beget at a hundred years, and there is in this no change in the way of the world, unlike the miracles performed by the Explicit Name (השם המיוחד) through our master Moses, peace be upon him, which are publicized miracles that alter the nature and custom of the world, such as the splitting of the Sea of Reeds and the descent of the manna and others — end of quotation. And one may explain the reason He mentioned this name for this commandment by noting that it ends with the letter yod (יו\"ד), and is portrayed in the shape of the body to be circumcised, sealed with the sign of holy covenant. And through this sign the distinction of Israel is publicized, and their being set apart from the rest of the uncircumcised nations (עו\"ג), uncircumcised of heart and uncircumcised of flesh, who are from the side of the demons (שדים), whereas Israel are from the side of El Shaddai. And through this sign a person is called תמים (whole) — this is what it says, והיה תמים. And since he is תמים within it, it is fitting that he guard himself from the sin that is unique to this matter, namely the sin of forbidden relations (עריות); and so it is written (II Samuel 22:24): ״ואהיה תמים עמו ואשתמרה מעוני״. And for the holiness of the covenants, the name יו\"ד ה\"א ו\"ו is bound and inscribed in a person, from below upward, and so too in this verse. And understand this." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״אני אל שדי״ — He said to him: If you accept circumcision, well and good; and if not, I say to the world: די (enough). If you do not accept circumcision, it is enough for the worlds — end of quotation. דיה לערלה — it is sufficient for the foreskin — end of quotation, in Bereshit Rabbah." "<b>And the explanation of this Midrash is</b> that the power of Israel, the covenant-bearing sons, lies in the heart. And the seventy princes are the outer shells (הקליפות החיצונות) — thirty-five on the right for merit and thirty-five on the left for liability — and this is the import of ערלה. And a person is not permitted to take hold of the heart until those shells are removed. And had Abraham not accepted circumcision, I would say: it is enough for the worlds, enough — meaning that neither the Shekhinah nor the princes would perform any action from that point on; the world would be nullified." "<b>Walk before.</b> You shall be drawn to My service. And והיה תמים — whole, without addition or diminution, in the manner of ״תורת ה' תמימה״; and it is written (Deuteronomy 13:1): ״לא תוסיף עליו ולא תגרע ממנו״." "<b>Know</b> that one is called תמים (whole) whose inside is as his outside, and whose mouth and heart are equal. And because Abraham our father, peace be upon him, was the root of faith and the head of all who declare unity, and the matter of unification (יחוד) requires that the testimony of the heart and the mouth be equal, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, mentioned concerning him ״והיה תמים״. The same applies to all other services of the Holy One, blessed be He: a person must have his mouth and heart equal, each justifying the other and testifying to it, not contradicting it in any way. For anyone who is one thing with his mouth and another with his heart — his part contradicts his part, and his part lies to his part — belongs to the faction about whom it is said (Psalms 78:36–37): ״ויפתוהו בפיהם ובלשונם יכזבו לו / ולבם לא נכון עמו ולא נאמנו בבריתו״. Therefore there is a positive commandment in the Torah, as it is said (Deuteronomy 18:13): ״תמים תהיה עם ה' אלהיך״. And this is the great praise spoken of all the righteous: ״איש צדיק תמים״. And it is written (Genesis 25:27): ״ויעקב איש תם״. And so Isaac is called ״עולה תמימה״. And it is written (Psalms 15:2): ״הולך תמים ופועל צדק״. And it is written (ibid. 101): ״אשכילך בדרך תמים״. And it is written (ibid. 119): ״אשרי תמימי דרך״. And David, peace be upon him, already informed us that one who possesses this trait stands before HaShem, blessed be He, established and eternal forever — this is what he said (ibid. 41): ״ואני בתומי תמכת בי ותציבני לפניך לעולם״. And Solomon said (Proverbs 10:9): ״הולך בתום ילך בטח״. And he said further (ibid. 11): ״ורצונו תמימי דרך״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {5 "The closing words 'וכן בפסוק זה, והבן זה' likely allude to the Kabbalistic inscription of the divine name in the form of circumcision; left untranslated in their veiled sense per the Kabbalah rail."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:18.523033240Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}